    "https://english.hi.is/about-ui/working-ui/vacancies#Assistant-Professor-in-Geochemistry-and-Environmental-Geology": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Geochemistry and Environmental Geology",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Geochemistry and Environmental Geology The Faculty of Earth Sciences at the University of Iceland invites applications for a full-time position as Assistant Professor in Geochemistry and Environmental Geology. According to the University of Iceland's regulations, academic duties are divided into three main areas: research (40%), teaching (48%), and administration (12%). Further information Field of work Teach undergraduate courses and courses at the graduate-level in the candidate's area of research specialization Supervision of undergraduate and graduate students Lead and contribute to research projects in geochemistry and environmental geoscience, utilizing Iceland's unique geological setting Contribute to the development and organization of academic programs and faculty administration Lead and contribute to the operation and development of geochemistry research laboratories Engage in collaborative projects within and outside the University Qualification requirements A doctoral degree in geochemistry, geology, or a related field Experience in university-level teaching and student supervision is an advantage Research activity in geochemistry and environmental geoscience, commensurate with career stage Excellent proficiency in English, both written and spoken Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to effectively share and disseminate knowledge Ability to teach in Icelandic is an advantage. The successful candidate is required to attain Icelandic language proficiency at B1 level within four years of appointment. The University of Iceland provides support for acquiring the necessary language skills In hiring decisions, considerations will be given to how well the applicant fits the current conditions and needs of the Faculty of Earth Sciences. Application process The expected starting date is no later than August 1st 2027. The position will be filled for a term of five years, with the possibility of a permanent appointment thereafter, cf. Paragraph 3, Article 31 of the Rules of the University of Iceland No. 569/2009. The processing of applications, evaluation of candidates ` qualifications, and appointment to the position shall follow the provisions of Act No. 85/2008 on Public Universities and the Rules of the University of Iceland No. 569/2009. Applicants must submit the following documents with their application: A cover letter outlining, among other things, how the applicant meets the qualification requirements Diploma(s) or, where applicable, a certificate of academic record Academic curriculum vitae (CV) List of publications A report on research work and other professional work undertaken A statement of research and teaching plans if appointed Information on potential referees who may be contacted The application must indicate which of the applicant ` s publications, up to eight in total, are considered most significant with regard to the position in question. Applicants should submit only these publications with their application or provide information on where they are electronically accessible. If a submitted work has multiple authors, applicants must clarify their individual contribution to the work. Application materials that cannot be submitted electronically should be sent in two copies to the Science and Innovation Department, University of Iceland, Sæmundargata 2, 101 Reykjavík. All applications will be answered, and applicants will be informed of the outcome once a decision has been made. Applications may remain valid for six months after the application deadline. In hiring for positions at the University of Iceland, the University ` s equality policy is taken into account. Attention is also drawn to the University of Iceland ` s language policy . Since 1911, the University of Iceland has been a leading institution for business and societal progress in Iceland. It is a forward-looking research university and an active participant in international scientific collaboration. The University of Iceland is the largest university in the country, offering nearly 400 programs at the undergraduate, master`s, and doctoral levels. Approximately 14,000 students are enrolled, including over 2,000 international students. More than 1,800 staff members work at the University of Iceland. The university emphasizes attracting a diverse community of employees and students to an international academic environment, where academic freedom, professionalism, equality, diversity, and inclusion are prioritized. About the University of Iceland Schools, faculties and interdisciplinary studies International Staff Services Application deadline Application deadline is 02.11.2026 For further information contact Andri Stefánsson as@hi.is Apply now"
    },
    "https://english.hi.is/about-ui/working-ui/vacancies#Assistant-Professor-of-Applied-Mathematics": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Applied Mathematics",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Applied Mathematics Applications are invited for a full-time position of Assistant Professor of Applied Mathematics in the Department of Mathematics, the Faculty of Physical Sciences, School of Engineering and Natural Sciences, University of Iceland.According to the University of Iceland's rules, the duties of academic staff are divided into three main components: research, teaching, and administration.Undergraduate teaching is generally in Icelandic. The successful candidate is expected to be able to teach in Icelandic after five years in the position. Further information Field of work Teaching and developing undergraduate and graduate courses in mathematics and applied mathematics Research in applied mathematics Supervising students' final projects Securing research grants Taking part in running the department and serving on committees for the faculty, the school or the university Qualification requirements A PhD in mathematics, applied mathematics or a related field University teaching experience International research activity in applied mathematics, shown among other things by publications in international journals Good spoken and written English Good spoken and written Icelandic is desirable Good collaboration and interpersonal skills The successful candidate should have a strong foundation in mathematics so they can teach courses in a wide range of areas, such as numerical analysis, theoretical statistics and general introductory mathematics courses. The needs and circumstances of the Department of Mathematics and the Faculty of Physical Sciences will be taken into account in the hiring decision. Application process The successful candidate is expected to start as soon as possible after the relevant committees have finished their work. The position will be filled for a term of five years, with the possibility of a permanent appointment thereafter, cf. Paragraph 3, Article 31 of the Rules of the University of Iceland No. 569/2009. The processing of applications, evaluation of candidates` qualifications, and appointment to the position shall follow the provisions of Act No. 85/2008 on Public Universities and the Rules of the University of Iceland No. 569/2009. Applicants must submit the following documents with their application: A cover letter outlining, among other things, how the applicant meets the qualification requirements Diploma(s) or, where applicable, a certificate of academic record Academic curriculum vitae (CV) List of publications A report on research work and other professional work undertaken A statement of research and teaching plans if appointed Information on three potential referees who may be contacted The application must indicate which of the applicant`s publications, up to eight in total, are considered most significant with regard to the position in question. Applicants should submit only these publications with their application or provide information on where they are electronically accessible. If a submitted work has multiple authors, applicants must clarify their individual contribution to the work. Application materials that cannot be submitted electronically should be sent in two copies to the Science and Innovation Department, University of Iceland, Sæmundargata 2, 101 Reykjavík. All applications will be answered, and applicants will be informed of the outcome once a decision has been made. Applications may remain valid for six months after the application deadline. In hiring for positions at the University of Iceland, the University`s equality policy is taken into account. Attention is also drawn to the University of Iceland`s language policy . Since 1911, the University of Iceland has been a leading institution for business and societal progress in Iceland. It is a forward-looking research university and an active participant in international scientific collaboration. The University of Iceland is the largest university in the country, offering nearly 400 programs at the undergraduate, master`s, and doctoral levels. Approximately 14,000 students are enrolled, including over 2,000 international students. More than 1,800 staff members work at the University of Iceland. The university emphasizes attracting a diverse community of employees and students to an international academic environment, where academic freedom, professionalism, equality, diversity, and inclusion are prioritized. About the University of Iceland Schools, faculties and interdisciplinary studies International Staff Services Application deadline Application deadline is 02.11.2026 For further information contact Anna Helga Jónsdóttir ahj@hi.is Ingibjörg Óðinsdóttir ingaodins@hi.is Apply now"
